## Your job

Implement **only step {{STEP_N}}**, defined in:
- `task/instruction.md` (root contract: product, exact seed data, formats, rules), and
- the step contract under `task/steps/` — step {{STEP_N}} is the only one present.

The root contract describes the finished product so you have context; anything beyond
step {{STEP_N}} is **not your job**. Your work is graded by {{GRADED_TESTS}} run on a
cloud iOS simulator against a build of the source you submit. Nothing past step
{{STEP_N}} is graded: building ahead earns nothing and costs agent time, which is
scored. Every submission is graded against **all** declared tests, so a change that
breaks an earlier step is caught. The run ends when all declared tests pass, when you
have used all **{{MAX_SUBMISSIONS}} submissions**, or at the wall clock
({{WALL_CLOCK_MIN}} min from start).

**A submission is a final-exam attempt, not a progress check.** Check your step with a
development build — the budget below is counted in builds. Submit only when you have verified on-device that step
{{STEP_N}} works and earlier steps still do. Expect your first submission to be your
only one.

## How your run is evaluated

In priority order:
1. **What passes** — the verdict vector of your final submission; completing your step
   dominates all other factors.
2. **How few submissions you used** — each extra submission meaningfully lowers your
   evaluation. Submitting to see what the hidden tests say is a losing strategy: the
   penalty for an extra submission always outweighs the time saved by skipping your own
   on-device verification.
3. **How little agent time you spent** — time you spend working (build/grading wait is
   excluded). Verifying on-device is time well spent; idling, flailing, and building
   unrequested steps are not.
Breaking something that previously passed (a regression) is penalized separately and
severely. Protocol violations (see Environment facts) can invalidate the run entirely.

Read `AGENTS.md` first. Commit your work in small, clear commits as you go (`git` is local-only; there is
no remote).

## Checkpoint your step (required — not a submission)

The moment you believe step {{STEP_N}} is complete — verified on a device, before
you submit — record it:

```bash
./checkpoint.sh {{STEP_N}}
```

This commits and tags your tree as the step boundary. It is NOT a submission: nothing is
graded, no feedback is returned, and it does not consume your submission budget or count
against you in any way. `./checkpoint.sh list` shows what you have recorded.

